[
  {
    "id": "MC-LSN-1.1",
    "number": "1.1",
    "title": "What's an argument?",
    "hook": "Hark, a simple argument. Before we hunt the conclusion, we have to know what kind of beast we're hunting.",
    "prose_blocks": [
      { "kind": "p", "text": "An argument is the author's viewpoint, opinion, or decision, supported by premises and evidence. It is not just a statement of fact, and it is not just an unsupported opinion. An argument is a claim plus the support that's been built up underneath it." },
      { "kind": "p", "text": "Imagine you're building a table made of blocks to keep a delicious piece of cake off the floor (you live a very interesting life in this example). Your blocks are solid. They're not going anywhere. These are your <em>premises</em>. Your cake is squishy and fragile. It needs to be held up off the floor because it can't fend for itself. This is your <em>conclusion</em>." },
      { "kind": "visual-spec", "component": "CakeOnBlocks", "caption": "Tap a premise block. Watch the conclusion fall." },
      { "kind": "p", "text": "The cake is only safe from the floor because you arranged your blocks exactly right. Conclusions are a little ridiculous like that too. They can't hold themselves up. If you left a conclusion to its own devices, it would turn into a hot mess on the floor. <strong>Conclusions need premises to be worth anything.</strong>" },
      { "kind": "p", "text": "Every LR argument has, at minimum, a premise and a main conclusion. Some arguments add an intermediate conclusion in between — a middle layer that has its own support and itself supports the main claim. We'll come back to that in Lesson 1.7. For now: claim plus support. That's the beast." },
      { "kind": "callout", "label": "The three pieces of an argument", "body": "<strong>Main Conclusion</strong> — the primary point the author is trying to convey.<br/><strong>Premise</strong> — the evidence that directly supports the conclusion.<br/><strong>Intermediate Conclusion</strong> — a statement supported by premises that itself supports the main conclusion. Optional. A link in the chain." },
      { "kind": "example", "tag": "Worked example — Monica claimed the dinosaurs (logicalreasoningfoundation prototype)", "body": "&ldquo;Monica claimed to have caused the extinction of the dinosaurs, and everything Monica claims is true. Therefore, Monica caused the extinction of the dinosaurs.&rdquo;<br/><br/><strong>Premises:</strong> Monica claimed it. Everything Monica claims is true.<br/><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Monica caused the extinction.<br/><br/>The conclusion is silly because the second premise is silly — but accept the premises and the structure works. On the LSAT, you accept the premises. Push back on the <em>conclusion</em>, not the support." },
      { "kind": "p", "text": "One more thing before we move on. Watch where the conclusion is hiding. School taught you it goes at the end. The LSAT writers know that. In more than 90% of Main Conclusion questions, the conclusion is somewhere else. We give that pattern a name in Lesson 1.4 — the [[NT-Upside-Down-Argument]]Upside Down Argument[[/]] — and once you can spot it, the question type collapses into a recognition exercise." }
    ],
    "named_tool_callouts": [{ "tool_id": "NT-Upside-Down-Argument", "appears_in_paragraph": 6 }],
    "reference_callouts": [
      { "reference_id": "MC-REF-2.B", "label": "2-Part Conclusion Check →" },
      { "reference_id": "MC-REF-2.A", "label": "Indicator Vault →" }
    ],
    "checkpoint": {
      "prompt": "Which is the conclusion?",
      "options": [
        { "id": "A", "text": "Monica claimed to have caused the extinction of the dinosaurs.", "correct": false, "reveal": "This is a premise. The argument treats it as given (no support is offered for it)." },
        { "id": "B", "text": "Everything Monica claims is true.", "correct": false, "reveal": "Also a premise. Notice nothing in the argument tries to defend this claim — the author just states it." },
        { "id": "C", "text": "Monica caused the extinction of the dinosaurs.", "correct": true, "reveal": "This is the conclusion — the supported claim. Both prior sentences point at it. The 'Therefore' is the giveaway, but the support test is what confirms it." }
      ]
    },
    "sources": [
