        body: '"I was shocked to learn that Judge Mosston was convicted of criminal assault. Nevertheless, despite his conviction, Mosston should not be forced to resign as judge. He has a long record of community service..."  Concession: "shocked... convicted." Pivot: "Nevertheless." Conclusion: "Mosston should not be forced to resign." Support: community service record.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'p',
        text: '11 of the canonical 20 are Rebuttal-family. Drill 3.4 stage 2 trains nothing but rebuttal recognition.',
      },
    ],
  },

  // ── 2.G · Three Incorrect-Answer Trap Types ──────────────────────────────
  {
    id: 'MC-REF-2.G',
    title: 'The Three Incorrect-Answer Trap Types (legacy gateway)',
    route: '/reference/three-traps',
    voice_register: 1,
    source: 'Notes/Main Conclusion Synopsis.pdf (S) + Notes/Advanced- MP Question Traps_dup1.docx (A)',
    status: 'authored',
    hook: 'A starter taxonomy. The full Trap Master expands these to seven traits — but the three patterns underneath every wrong answer live here.',
    blocks: [
      {
        kind: 'h2',
        text: 'The three patterns',
      },
      {
        kind: 'list',
        ordered: true,
        items: [
          'Premise as conclusion — the answer lifts a support sentence and floats it as the claim. The most common trap on First-sentence stimuli.',
          'Out of scope — the answer generalizes the claim or imports a topic the stimulus did not address.',
          'Too strong — the answer says something the author would agree with but in a stronger form (more universal, more certain, more specific) than the stimulus warrants.',
        ],
      },
      {
        kind: 'callout',
        label: 'How this maps to the seven Trap Master traits',
        body: 'T1 absorbs Too strong. T2 absorbs Premise as conclusion. T6 absorbs Out of scope. T3, T4, T5, T7 add finer distinctions. Start with three. Graduate to seven once those click.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'p',
        text: 'On every wrong answer, name the pattern. "Premise-as-conclusion" — done, move on. The naming itself is the diagnostic.',
      },
    ],
  },

  // ── 2.H · Pronoun & Reference Library ────────────────────────────────────
  {
    id: 'MC-REF-2.H',
    title: 'Pronoun & Reference Library',
    route: '/reference/pronoun-library',
    voice_register: 1,
    source: 'Notes/Main Conclusion Synopsis.pdf (S) + Notes/main_conclusion_role_merged_note.pdf (S)',
    status: 'authored',
    hook: 'Replace the pronoun before you say the conclusion to yourself. Then check the answer choices.',
    blocks: [
      {
        kind: 'h2',
        text: 'Words to scan for',
      },
      {
        kind: 'list',
        items: [
          'this · that · these · those',
          'they · them · their · its',
          'such · this position · this view · these laws · such laws',
          'the policy · the recommendation · the practice (when the antecedent isn’t named in the same sentence)',
        ],
      },
      {
        kind: 'callout',
        label: 'The Pre-Phrase Goal',
        body: 'When you formulate the conclusion in your own words before checking answers, you must replace every pronoun with the actual reference language from the stimulus. Otherwise the answer choices will look more similar than they are.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'example',
        label: 'Why replacement matters',
        body: 'Conclusion (with pronoun): "This position should not stand." Without replacement, three answer choices look right. With replacement: "The view that Mosston should resign should not stand." Now only one answer matches.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'p',
        text: 'Drill 3.7 trains pronoun replacement on the canonical 20 stimuli. After ten reps it becomes automatic.',
      },
    ],
  },

  // ── 2.I · Concession Decoder ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    id: 'MC-REF-2.I',
    title: 'The Concession Decoder',
    route: '/reference/concession-decoder',
    voice_register: 1,
    source: 'Notes/Argument Core & Periph_dup1.pdf (A) + Notes/Skeletal Breakdown LR (1)_dup1.pdf (A) + Notes/Indicator word list .pdf (A)',
    status: 'authored',
    hook: 'Concessions look like agreement. They are setting up the rebuttal.',
    blocks: [
      {
        kind: 'h2',
        text: 'Concession words',
      },
      {
        kind: 'list',
        items: [
          'granted',
          'admittedly',
          'although',
          'while',
          'despite',
          'even though',
          'it is true that',
          'of course',
          'no doubt',
        ],
      },
      {
        kind: 'callout',
        label: 'What concessions do',
        body: 'They temporarily inhabit the opposing view. The author is signaling: "I see what you would object with — let me name it briefly so I can refute it." The actual claim follows the pivot.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'h2',
        text: 'The structural reveal',
      },
      {
        kind: 'p',
        text: 'Concession → Pivot → Author’s claim → Support. When you see a concession word, the conclusion is not the next sentence. It is the sentence after the pivot.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'example',
        label: 'In practice',
        body: '"Granted, the program has had setbacks. But its long-term outcomes have justified the investment, and continued funding is warranted."  Concession: "setbacks." Pivot: "But." Conclusion: "continued funding is warranted." The "long-term outcomes" sentence is support.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'p',
        text: 'On every Rebuttal-family stimulus, find the concession first. The conclusion is downstream of the pivot. Always.',
      },
    ],
  },

  // ── 2.J · Quick Reference Card ───────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    id: 'MC-REF-2.J',
    title: 'Quick Reference Card (printable)',
    route: '/reference/quick-card',
    voice_register: 1,
    source: '(composite — single page; spec.html L1969)',
    status: 'authored',
    hook: 'One page. Print it. Tape it to the desk.',
    blocks: [
      {
        kind: 'h2',
        text: 'The 2-Part Conclusion Check',
      },
      {
        kind: 'list',
        ordered: true,
        items: [
          'Does it sound like the author’s opinion?',
          'Is it supported by at least one explicit claim?',
        ],
      },
      {
        kind: 'h2',
        text: 'FABS',
      },
      {
        kind: 'p',
        text: 'For · After all · Because · Since. Whatever follows is support; the conclusion is next door.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'h2',
        text: 'The two families',
      },
      {
        kind: 'list',
        items: [
          'First-sentence: claim first, support after.',
          'Rebuttal: concession → pivot → claim → support.',
        ],
      },
      {
        kind: 'h2',
        text: 'The seven trap traits',
      },
      {
        kind: 'list',
        items: [
          'T1 — Too strong (specifies a remedy or universal the author did not commit to)',
          'T2 — Premise as conclusion (lifts a support sentence)',
          'T3 — Concession as conclusion (Trojan horse — sounds like the claim, the author is conceding)',
          'T4 — Half the claim (captures part, drops the operative phrase)',
          'T5 — Restated stimulus (paraphrases neutrally without identifying the load-bearing claim)',
          'T6 — Out of scope (generalizes or imports a foreign topic)',
          'T7 — Intermediate conclusion (a sub-claim that supports the main one but is not the main one)',
        ],
      },
      {
        kind: 'h2',
        text: 'Pre-Phrase Protocol',
      },
      {
        kind: 'list',
        ordered: true,
        items: [
          'Read the stimulus.',
          'Find the conclusion (FABS / 2-Part Check / family-recognition).',
          'State it in your own words. Replace every pronoun with the antecedent.',
          'Compare the answer choices.',
        ],
      },
      {
        kind: 'p',
        text: 'Print double-sided. Register 1 on this side. The Indicator Vault (six categories with example words) on the other.',
      },
    ],
  },

  // ── 2.K · Reference-as-Companion ─────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    id: 'MC-REF-2.K',
    title: 'Reference-as-Companion (capstone — implicit)',
    route: '/reference/companion-mode',
    voice_register: 1,
    source: '(behavior — RightDrawer overlay handler; not a standalone page)',
    status: 'authored',
    hook: 'The Reference is not a destination. It is a companion that pops in beside whatever you are working on.',
    blocks: [
      {
        kind: 'p',
        text: 'Open any reference section while inside a lesson, drill, or simulator question and it surfaces in the right drawer in place — no URL change, no scroll loss. Press Esc to dismiss. The companion exists so you never have to leave the work to look something up.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'callout',
        label: 'How to invoke',
        body: 'Cmd+K opens the command palette. Type any inventory ID (MC-REF-2.B, NT-FABS, T3) and the matching surface drops into the right drawer. The keyboard cheat sheet in Settings lists every shortcut.',
      },
      {
        kind: 'p',
        text: 'This is not a feature. It is the architectural posture: the curriculum is the surface, the reference is the air, and you should never feel the seam between them.',
      },
    ],
  },
];

